No stack given. Assume Node/TypeScript + Express + Redis. Pattern: **cache-aside** with TTL, invalidate on write, stampede guard, negative caching.

## `cache.ts`

```typescript
import Redis from "ioredis";

const redis = new Redis(process.env.REDIS_URL ?? "redis://localhost:6379");

const TTL_SECONDS = 300;          // 5 min fresh
const NEGATIVE_TTL_SECONDS = 30;  // cache "not found" briefly
const LOCK_TTL_MS = 5000;
const NULL_SENTINEL = "__null__";

const key = (userId: string) => `user:profile:v1:${userId}`;
const lockKey = (userId: string) => `lock:${key(userId)}`;

// Jitter TTL so keys don't all expire together
const jitter = (ttl: number) => ttl + Math.floor(Math.random() * ttl * 0.1);

export async function getCachedProfile<T>(
  userId: string,
  loader: (id: string) => Promise<T | null>
): Promise<T | null> {
  const k = key(userId);

  // 1. Try cache (fail open if Redis down)
  try {
    const hit = await redis.get(k);
    if (hit !== null) return hit === NULL_SENTINEL ? null : (JSON.parse(hit) as T);
  } catch (err) {
    console.warn("cache read failed, falling through", err);
    return loader(userId);
  }

  // 2. Stampede guard: one request rebuilds, others wait briefly
  const gotLock = await redis
    .set(lockKey(userId), "1", "PX", LOCK_TTL_MS, "NX")
    .catch(() => null);

  if (!gotLock) {
    for (let i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
      await new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, 50));
      const hit = await redis.get(k).catch(() => null);
      if (hit !== null) return hit === NULL_SENTINEL ? null : (JSON.parse(hit) as T);
    }
    // Lock holder slow — load ourselves
    return loader(userId);
  }

  // 3. Load from source, populate cache
  try {
    const value = await loader(userId);
    const payload = value === null ? NULL_SENTINEL : JSON.stringify(value);
    const ttl = value === null ? NEGATIVE_TTL_SECONDS : jitter(TTL_SECONDS);
    await redis.set(k, payload, "EX", ttl).catch(() => {});
    return value;
  } finally {
    await redis.del(lockKey(userId)).catch(() => {});
  }
}

export async function invalidateProfile(userId: string): Promise<void> {
  await redis.del(key(userId));
}
```

## `routes.ts`

```typescript
import express from "express";
import { getCachedProfile, invalidateProfile } from "./cache";
import { db } from "./db"; // your data layer

const router = express.Router();

interface UserProfile {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  avatarUrl: string;
  bio: string;
}

router.get("/users/:id/profile", async (req, res) => {
  const profile = await getCachedProfile<UserProfile>(req.params.id, (id) =>
    db.users.findProfile(id)
  );
  if (!profile) return res.status(404).json({ error: "not found" });

  res.set("Cache-Control", "private, max-age=60"); // browser cache, not CDN
  res.json(profile);
});

router.put("/users/:id/profile", async (req, res) => {
  // authz check here (user can only edit own profile)
  const updated = await db.users.updateProfile(req.params.id, req.body);
  await invalidateProfile(req.params.id); // delete AFTER DB commit
  res.json(updated);
});

export default router;
```

## Key decisions

- **Delete on write, not update** — avoids race where stale write overwrites fresh. Delete after DB commit.
- **`v1` in key** — bump version when profile shape changes; old keys age out.
- **Fail open** — Redis outage → hit DB, not 500.
- **Negative cache** — blocks repeated lookups of nonexistent IDs hammering DB.
- **`private` Cache-Control** — profile may contain user-specific data; CDN must not share it. If profile public, `public, s-maxage=60` OK.
- **Jitter** — prevents synchronized expiry spikes.

## Watch out

- Multi-instance writers + replicas: replica lag can repopulate stale data right after delete. Fix: delayed second delete (~1s) or read from primary on miss.
- Don't cache sensitive fields (email, tokens) unless needed; Redis often less guarded than DB.
- Metrics: track hit ratio, `get` latency, lock-wait fallbacks.

